  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nCold front is slowly pushing eastward across North Dakota, and\nhas stalled around central North Dakota. Abundant wildfire smoke\nhas moved in behind this front and has lowered visibilities\nacross the west. These visibilities will remain reduced through\nat least tonight, and cold linger through much of the day\nMonday. Ahead of this front remains hot temperatures and modest\ndewpoints. For the most part these are contained within the Heat\nAdvisory. Some counties just north and east of this Heat\nAdvisory are close to criteria. With the front progressing \neastward, these heat index values should be peaking soon. Breezy\nsouth winds are also found ahead of this front, with some \nbreezy northwest winds along and behind this front. Low RH \nvalues combined with these breezy winds are bringing some \ncritical fire weather conditions across the west. The Red Flag \nWarning remains as a result, see the Fire Weather Discussion \nbelow for more details. For the rest of the night, most CAMs \ndelay convection until later tonight, although the latest \nNAMNest tries to initiate convection in the more moist central \nareas this evening. MUCAPE and shear in these areas are somewhat\nrobust this evening, although surface based cape is quite \ncapped. Thus anything that does develop this evening would be \nelevated, although could be strong to severe with hail to \nquarters and winds to 60 mph the main threat. The more increased\nchances for showers and thunderstorms still look to be later \ntonight through Monday morning as the surface low takes better \nshape and the upper wave moves threat. Same threats are possible\nand SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk for central and eastern \nareas tonight. \n\nMonday, critical fire weather conditions will remain across\nsouthwestern ND, and spread slightly eastward into south central\nportions, mainly west of the Missouri River. This stems from\nbreezy north winds and low humidity values behind today's front\ndespite some cooler temperatures. See the Fire Weather\nDiscussion below for more details. Otherwise the surface low\nwill continue to push eastward, with the cold front reinforced\nand also pushing eastward. The result could be periods of\nshowers and thunderstorms, especially in the central and east.\nSPC has maintained a Marginal to Slight Risk for the eastern\nthird of North Dakota. There could be around of strong to severe\nstorms in the morning, then another round in the afternoon and\nevening. The afternoon and evening storms may have more shear to\nwork with and could be better organized to perhaps supercell\nstorm mode. Higher chances for storms are just east of the CWA,\nyet any stalling of the front could shift this into the James\nRiver Valley and will need to be monitored. Otherwise look for\ncooler temperatures on Monday with highs near 70 northwest to\nmid 80s southeast. North northwest winds could get close to\nadvisory level behind this front on Monday, especially with\nsustained wind speeds. Wildfire smoke may also linger Monday,\ndiminishing somewhat Monday night. Precipitation chances also \ndiminish Monday, with cool lows in the 40s west to 50s east.\n\nForecast confidence remains high that surface high pressure \nwill build into the region on Tuesday under an upper level \nlongwave trough. This should bring below normal temperatures to \nall, with highs on Tuesday in the 70s and lows around 45 to 50\nTuesday night. A quick warm up remains favored thereafter, with\nnear normal highs in the 80s on Wednesday increasing to above \nnormal in the 90s along and south and west of the Missouri River\nby Friday. But there is still uncertainty in the evolution of \nthe synoptic pattern and how much the Hudson Bay low may keep \nthe western CONUS upper ridge from building eastward. This \nuncertainty is reflected in larger temperature spreads by the \nend of the work week, and also keeps precipitation chances after\nMonday very low for the time being. The northwest flow pattern\naloft could provide for some breezy conditions through at least\nmid week. This is of note as dry conditions look to linger\nthrough the week, with afternoon RH values forecast in the teens\nand 20s, with some 30s. This could bring some increased fire\nweather concerns through the week, depending on if the breezier\nwinds align with the lower humidity values.",
